{
  "abstract": "Let u_1 < u_2 < ... be the positive integers with at most two prime factors, counted with multiplicity. Erdős Problem 1139 asks whether limsup (u_{k+1} - u_k) / log k = infinity. This formalization proves that it is, in the formulation of google-deepmind/formal-conjectures (Erdos1139.erdos_1139). The proof is an Erdős-Rankin style construction: the Chinese remainder theorem gives N such that every number in a long interval N + 1, ..., N + y is given a divisor below it with exactly two prime factors (two assigned primes, or the square of one), and so has at least three prime factors, with log N small compared to y. The residues of the small primes are chosen by averaging over all assignments, with Euler-product weights and smooth-number bounds; larger primes are grouped into bands and handled by a second-moment argument; the few remaining numbers are patched with squares of reserve primes. No results on linear equations in primes are used.",
  "authors": [
